        ".land's edge is its double reading. The literal layer is land: real-estate and land marketplaces, farms and agriculture ventures, campgrounds and outdoor destinations — name.land says it outright. The figurative layer is worlds: English builds \"a place of X\" with -land (think Disneyland), so game worlds, virtual communities and metaverse projects wear it with built-in imagination, plus the domain-hack bonus (wonderland → wonder.land). In these niches the same roots are long hoarded or premium-priced on .com while .land sits wide open at mid-tier prices (about {{price:land:first:usd}} to register, {{price:land:renew:usd}}/yr to renew). .com's case remains the classic two: autocomplete reflex and the best long-term asset liquidity — mainstream brands, and any business unrelated to land, space or worlds, should still default to .com; forcing .land there just reads as random. The test: if your business or brand story carries the imagery of a place → .land's semantics and inventory are pure upside; if not → stay in .com's safety zone. On the registry side the contrast is old versus new: .com is Verisign's flagship, in the root since 1985 and priced under a long-running ICANN registry agreement; .land is a November 2013 new gTLD — among the earliest delegations of the 2013 new-gTLD round — sponsored by Binky Moon, LLC on the Identity Digital platform, with registry-set pricing and no eligibility rules. Being first-wave has a quiet advantage: .land has more than a decade of clean operation behind it, so mail providers and browsers treat it as an ordinary gTLD rather than a novelty. For property businesses there is a compliance angle .com cannot offer: land and real-estate marketing is regulated in many jurisdictions, and a suffix that literally says land helps disclosures read as intentional rather than generic. The world-building reading also gives .land a natural home for map-based products — a hiking-trail app, a farmland marketplace, a survival game — where .com would force a longer compound. Use-case split: a rural acreage brokerage, an agritourism farm, a hex-map strategy game → .land; a national homebuilder with TV advertising, a mortgage lender, or any brand whose name already contains land (which would read as a stutter on .land) → .com.",
