      verdict:
        "The two overlap in gaming (LOL is both the laugh and League of Legends) but wear completely different vibes. .lol is the meme: run by XYZ Registry, it's the internet's universal laugh — meme and joke sites, comedy video projects, prank and April Fools' pages get a built-in punchline where the domain is part of the content; at about {{price:lol:first:usd}} for year one and {{price:lol:renew:usd}}/yr to renew, a gag page costs nothing, with a bonus pun for League communities. .gg is standard issue: born from the esports courtesy \"good game\" and cemented by Discord (discord.gg) as part of gaming's infrastructure, it's the recognized formal choice for game studios, esports teams and events, gaming tools and communities — it says \"serious about games\", not \"joking\". The price is the price: .gg is Guernsey's ccTLD and costs noticeably more to register and renew (tens of dollars a year, no teaser). The test: if humor and memes are the content → .lol's punchline and bargain price are unmatched; building a real game product, team, tool or community → .gg's insider trust earns its cost. Cross them and you lose both ways: a serious game on .lol reads unprofessional, a gag page on .gg wastes money. Teams with budget take both: .gg as the formal front door, .lol for meme marketing. Their registries explain the price gap better than any table. IANA lists .lol as a generic TLD delegated on 23 April 2015 to XYZ.COM LLC of Las Vegas, with CentralNic as technical contact; the registry's own site, nic.lol, pitches the suffix to comedy shows, meme sites and entertainment clubs and suggests city.lol for local comedy venues — a mass-market keyword run by a volume registry. .gg is the country code of Guernsey, delegated on 7 August 1996 and managed by Island Networks Ltd. from Alderney, one of the Channel Islands; as a ccTLD it answers to that manager's policy rather than to ICANN's generic-TLD contract, and a small-island registry has no incentive to chase volume with discounts. That is why .gg behaves like a scarce, stable asset and .lol like a promotional one. Use-case split: humour-first projects that benefit from a registry actively courting comedy — sketch channels, meme archives, comedy venues, prank campaigns → .lol; gaming organisations that need a tightly held, insider-recognised ccTLD and can carry its cost for years — teams, tournaments, stats tools, Discord-adjacent services → .gg.",
