      verdict:
        "Both suffixes have special chemistry in the Chinese internet context. .red is literally the color, but in Chinese it auto-loads associations of festivity, prosperity and internet fame — gift brands, wedding services, guochao (China-chic) labels and red-themed content wear it beautifully; among the Donuts-wave gTLDs its registration base is small, so good words remain plentiful at moderate prices. The catch: outside Chinese contexts, red is just a color and carries no extra meaning for international users. .vip is three letters the whole world reads the same way: membership systems, premium services and upscale positioning are instantly legible, its registration volume has long ranked near the top of new gTLDs in China, it's MIIT-accredited for ICP filing, and the member-entrance pattern (brand.vip) is battle-tested in e-commerce campaigns; the cost is a promotional flavor that reads try-hard on brands aiming for restraint. The test: your brand feeds on festive-red emotional energy (gifts, weddings, guochao, seasonal campaigns) → .red is charming and cheap; your brand sells membership and prestige (member-based commerce, clubs, premium services) → .vip is more direct with broader recognition. Both are heavily scenario-dependent — generic businesses should return to a neutral suffix. The registries pull in different directions. .red was delegated in January 2014 and is sponsored directly by Identity Digital Limited — one of the colour suffixes that sit in the portfolio without a Binky Moon or Dog Beach holding entity in between — and it is a quiet namespace where single colour-plus-noun words are far more likely to be open than on a busy suffix. .vip was delegated in July 2015 and is sponsored by Registry Services, LLC with GoDaddy Registry as technical operator; GoDaddy's own registry hub pitches it as a domain for everything exclusive and a red-carpet experience, and its showcase features subscription and luxury-hospitality brands, so the intended reading is prestige and membership. The two words also travel differently across languages. Red is a colour word that changes in every language (rouge, rot, rojo, 红), so a .red brand reads as English wordplay outside the anglosphere; vip is an initialism that has become a loanword almost everywhere, including in Chinese consumer culture where member tiers are routinely branded vip. Use-case split: a brand whose identity is literally the colour (a red-label product line, a wine, a lipstick brand), a political or activist campaign that owns red as a signal, a sports team in red, a design studio playing on the word → .red; a members-only club, a paid tier behind a login, a concierge or luxury-hospitality brand, a fan-club shop, a campaign aimed at Chinese-speaking consumers → .vip. If restraint is the brand, note that .red is a colour and .vip is a claim — the colour is easier to live up to.",
      pickA: ["Festive gift & wedding brands", "Guochao & red-themed content", "Seasonal campaign pages", "Chinese-context festive readings", "Identities that are literally the colour"],
