      verdict:
        "Both live in the movement economy; one names a practice, the other an industry. .yoga is as vertical as it gets — studios, teacher personal brands, trainings, online courses, meditation and mindfulness apps on name.yoga tell the audience exactly what you do; the register is breath, stretch and calm, and pilates and mind-body brands borrow it naturally; run by GoDaddy Registry at a flat {{price:yoga:renew:usd}}/yr for registration and renewal — no teaser, no trap; and yoga is a global word with zero translation cost for international teacher-training brands. .fitness spans the floor — gyms, CrossFit boxes, group classes, online programs and apps on name.fitness flex venue and strength; about {{price:fitness:first:usd}} to register and a mild {{price:fitness:renew:usd}}/yr to renew. The test: the business is yoga (or a mind-body practice borrowing its calm) → .yoga's vertical precision is unmatched, and at four letters it's shorter too; the business is general fitness, strength training, or plans to widen the offering → .fitness's industry breadth is safer — add classes or personal training without changing domains. Watch the mismatch in both directions: a pure yoga studio on .fitness dilutes the calm; a full gym on .yoga misleads outright. Shared notes: the industry wins students on social and word of mouth, so the domain is the brand site, schedule and booking home; and hot words (flow, om, fit) are heavily registered on both — check before you fall in love. Different operators sit behind the two words. IANA lists .yoga as a generic TLD delegated on 15 August 2014 and sponsored by Registry Services, LLC of Tempe, Arizona — GoDaddy Registry — whose product page carries the line help the world find balance with .yoga and courts studios and teachers directly. .fitness was delegated on 11 April 2014 to Binky Moon, LLC, the Identity Digital vehicle in Bellevue, Washington, one line in a portfolio of dozens of keyword suffixes. Two operators mean two premium-name lists and two promotional calendars, so a studio name priced as premium on one may be standard on the other, and first-year offers rarely coincide. There is also a naming-convention difference: yoga has been adopted unchanged into most world languages, so a .yoga address needs no translation for an international teacher-training brand, whereas fitness competes with local-language equivalents in many markets. Use-case split: a teacher, studio or retreat whose practice is yoga or a close relative and whose students may be anywhere in the world → .yoga; a gym, box or training brand whose offering is broader than one practice and whose members are mostly local → .fitness.",
      pickA: ["Yoga studios", "Teacher brands & trainings", "Online courses & meditation apps", "Pilates & mind-body brands", "Teacher-training brands with students worldwide"],
