      metaDescription:
        ".coffee names the thing; .cafe names the place. Compare the two coffee suffixes on semantics, pricing and fit, then hunt names available on both.",
      verdict:
        "Same cup, two viewpoints. .coffee names the thing — roasteries, bean and gear e-commerce, subscriptions, review sites and content communities on name.coffee sell coffee itself rather than any one shop; English keeps \"let's grab coffee\" as the universal social invite, a layer chat products borrow cleverly; run by Identity Digital at about {{price:coffee:first:usd}} to register and {{price:coffee:renew:usd}}/yr to renew, the mild tier among industry suffixes. .cafe names the place — independent coffee shops, bakeries, cat and book cafés on name.cafe match the shop sign exactly, with menu, booking and delivery links printing naturally; \"cafe\" also doubles online as the cozy gathering spot, so reading circles and developer communities run warmer on name.cafe than on .com; about {{price:cafe:first:usd}} to register, with renewal near {{price:cafe:renew:usd}}/yr a notch higher. The test could not be simpler: selling beans, subscriptions or content → .coffee points at the thing; a space that welcomes people (a physical shop or an online living room) → .cafe points at the place. Brands running both lines split cleanly: the shop on name.cafe, the beans and e-commerce on name.coffee — one brand, two doors, no conflict. Shared notes: coffee doubles two letters (ff, ee) so spell it out when spoken, while cafe's four letters cost nothing; and local shops win customers on maps and social, so the domain is the brand site and the store or booking front door. Both suffixes come off the same shelf: IANA lists .coffee (delegated 19 December 2013) and .cafe (delegated 12 March 2015) as generic TLDs sponsored by Binky Moon, LLC, with Identity Digital Limited as technical contact, so registration rules and renewal behaviour are the same and neither side has a registry-level advantage. The fifteen-month gap matters more than it looks. .coffee opened during the first wave of keyword suffixes, so bean-and-brand names were snapped up by roasters and equipment makers early; .cafe arrived later and speaks a different language — café is spelled the same way across French, Spanish, Portuguese and Italian menus, which makes it the more portable choice for a venue that serves customers in several languages. Use-case split: the product is the bean — roasting, subscriptions, brewing gear, coffee journalism → .coffee; the product is the room — a neighbourhood venue, a co-working café, a bakery-café chain naming each location → .cafe.",
