      metaDescription:
        ".family writes the household into the address; .com is the default suffix of universal trust. Compare semantics, pricing and naming patterns, then hunt names available on both.",
      verdict:
        "The split is warmth. .family is the belonging word — family homepages and photo archives, parenting content and communities, family-business sites: surname + .family is the standard pattern for a household's digital home, and smith.family-style names long gone on .com nearly always hit here. .com is the default word — what users autocomplete by reflex, where formal commercial brands and mass-market products pay zero trust tax. On price, .family runs about {{price:family:first:usd}} to register and {{price:family:renew:usd}}/yr to renew — the mild tier, painless to hold; .com sits around {{price:com:renew:usd}} both ways with flat pricing and the best resale liquidity of any suffix. The test: the subject is a household or a family narrative — family sites, genealogy, family businesses, parenting brands → .family carries a warmth .com can't; the subject is a formal commercial brand reaching the widest audience → .com's recognition is irreplaceable. Two cautions: family sites carry children's photos, so pair the domain with access control rather than going fully public; and .family is six letters and less recognized outside English markets, so serious commercial plays should hold the .com as backup. Naming: surname + .family (the smith.family homepage pattern) versus brand word + .com are each side's on-target patterns. The registries sit at opposite ends of the industry. .com is Verisign's zone, in the root since 1985 under an ICANN registry agreement most recently renewed in December 2024, with price terms fixed by amendment. .family was delegated in August 2015 to Dog Beach, LLC and runs on Identity Digital's platform under standard new-gTLD policy, with no eligibility rule — anyone may register a .family name, which matters because the word invites two very different buyers: households and businesses that sell to them. For a household the suffix is close to ideal: surname.family reads as exactly what it is, and the .com for most surnames was taken decades ago, so the namespace is genuinely open. For a business the calculation is different. Customers who see brand.family understand a values statement, but they also expect the brand to behave like a family business — small, personal, local — and a venture-backed company selling family-plan subscriptions may find the suffix promises an intimacy it cannot deliver. Use-case split: a family website with photos and a calendar, a reunion or genealogy project, a family foundation, a small family-run farm or restaurant that trades on being one, a parenting community → .family; a consumer brand with a family-plan tier, a childcare or education chain, an insurer or bank marketing family products, a media company with a family-friendly label → .com, where the neutral suffix lets the product speak. Households that also run a business under the surname often keep surname.family private and put the business on its own .com.",
