      verdict:
        "The split is the shape of the business. .fit is the slogan word — fitness apps and training-plan tools, personal-trainer brands, activewear stores on name.fit: fixed phrases like get fit and stay fit make verb + .fit read as a slogan, three letters travel best in app names and spoken mentions, and fit also means well-fitting — a built-in double meaning for apparel. .fitness is the gym word — gyms, yoga and pilates studios, CrossFit boxes, online course programs on name.fitness spell out the industry in full, with the formality a physical venue wants. Both price mild: .fit about {{price:fit:first:usd}} to register and {{price:fit:renew:usd}}/yr to renew — painless to hold; .fitness about {{price:fitness:first:usd}} and {{price:fitness:renew:usd}}/yr — a rounding error against membership revenue, so budget decides nothing here. The test: the subject is an app, a person or a slogan — fitness apps, trainer brands, activewear → .fit's brevity and double meaning win; the subject is a venue and its programs — physical gyms, course systems → .fitness reads more formal and is easier for local members verifying the name. Two cautions: .fit's {{price:fit:first:usd}} entry drew spam sites historically, so some platforms scrutinize .fit links — quality content carries the reputation; and fitness businesses win clients on short video and local platforms, so the domain's job is the stable booking front door. Naming: verb + .fit (the get.fit slogan pattern) versus venue or style word + .fitness (the standard gym pattern) are each side's on-target patterns. The operators differ, and so do their agendas. IANA lists .fit as a generic TLD delegated on 18 December 2014 and sponsored by Registry Services, LLC of Tempe, Arizona — GoDaddy Registry — whose product page for the suffix carries the line getting .fit starts today and courts trainers, apps and apparel brands. .fitness was delegated earlier, on 11 April 2014, to Binky Moon, LLC, the Identity Digital vehicle in Bellevue, Washington, and is one line in a portfolio of dozens of keyword suffixes. Two operators mean two premium-name lists and two promotional calendars, so a trainer's name or a slogan may be premium-priced on one and standard on the other; check both before committing. Length and language also pull differently — fit is three letters and doubles as an adjective in English but means little elsewhere, while fitness has been borrowed into most European languages and reads as an industry term everywhere. Use-case split: an English-language brand built on a slogan or an app icon, where three letters and the get-fit pun do the work → .fit; a venue or programme brand that must be understood by non-English speakers or that wants the full industry noun in the address → .fitness.",
