      verdict:
        "Both point at company, with opposite vibes. .ltd is the letterhead — company name + .ltd lines up with the certificate and reads properly registered limited company, a fit for SMEs and professional-services firms that want compliance in the address. .co is the brand shorthand — two letters, short and quick, long established as the mainstream alternative to .com in startup and fundraising circles; it reads a modern company, not a registration document. On price, .ltd runs about {{price:ltd:first:usd}} to register and {{price:ltd:renew:usd}}/yr to renew; .co registers around {{price:co:first:usd}} and renews at about {{price:co:renew:usd}}/yr — long-term costs land close, so the real difference is year one and vibe. The test: should the domain signal a compliant entity or a brand? Consulting firms, agencies and group subsidiaries that trade on credentials and trust → .ltd's letterhead feel is upside; product-led startups wanting a short domain and a fundraising-friendly read → .co travels lighter, with better inventory and resale liquidity for a two-letter suffix. Two cautions: .co is Colombia's country code globally rebranded, and a slice of users will fat-finger .com — buy the matching .com once the brand has traction; and keep .ltd off content sites and personal projects. Naming: company name + .ltd matches the certificate; brand word + .co as the main site is the standard on the other side. The registry records draw a sharp line. .ltd is a generic TLD delegated in September 2015 to Over Corner, LLC and now held by Binky Moon, LLC c/o Identity Digital, which lists it in its Business row beside .llc, .company and .group; anyone may register, and the string carries no company-registry check. .co is Colombia's country code — IANA shows a registration date of December 1991, a redelegation to .CO Internet SAS in November 2009 that started its global marketing, and a transfer of the ccTLD to Colombia's Ministry of Information and Communications Technologies (MinTIC) in September 2020. The registry's own site adds that day-to-day operation has been handled since 3 October 2025 by CentralNic Ltd, a Team Internet subsidiary, and that restricted names such as gov.co, edu.co and mil.co must go through the local registrar registrocolombia.co. So .ltd is governed by an ICANN base agreement, .co by Colombian policy plus a contracted operator — a difference in who can change the rules. Use-case split: a registered limited company that wants its legal form in the address, a holding entity, or a corporate site where the suffix should read as paperwork → .ltd; a consumer brand, an app or a startup that wants the two-letter shorthand and accepts ccTLD governance → .co.",
