  "computer-vs-tech": {
    slug: "computer-vs-tech",
    a: "computer",
    b: "tech",
    zh: {
      title: ".computer 和 .tech 怎么选：硬件维修具体词与科技泛称的分工",
      metaDescription: ".computer 具体指向电脑硬件——维修店、装机与二手翻新；.tech 是科技行业泛称，适合创业公司与开发者社区。对比两者的语义、价格与适用场景，并用 AI 猎取两个后缀下都可注册的名字。",
      verdict:
        "都是科技词，一个具体到「这台机器」，一个泛指「这个行业」。.computer 的语义具体而实在——电脑维修与上门服务、装机与硬件定制工作室、二手电脑与翻新交易、电脑培训与技能课程用 name.computer，「地名 + .computer」（brooklyn.computer 类）对本地维修店就是一句获客广告；也有独立开发者拿它做个人站玩复古计算机情怀的用法。注册约 {{price:computer:first:usd}}（{{price:computer:first:cny}}）、续费约 {{price:computer:renew:usd}}/年（{{price:computer:renew:cny}}），首年中档、续费温和，价格结构健康，长期持有无压力。.tech 是行业泛称——初创公司、开发者社区、黑客马拉松、科技媒体用 name.tech，后缀本身就是行业声明，认知度被 ces.tech 这类真实案例验证；注册便宜但续费明显高于首年促销价，长期持有前先算账。判断标准：业务围绕「电脑这台设备」——修、装、卖、教 → .computer 的具体语义比泛称更准、对本地客户更直白；业务是更广义的「科技」——软件、互联网、创业公司 → .tech 的行业泛称与圈内认知度更配。注意 .computer 语义窄，软件与互联网产品用它反而错位；.tech 则相反，具体到硬件维修时不如 .computer 点题。命名上「地名/服务 + .computer」接本地硬件生意，「品牌 + .tech」接科技公司。归属解释了两者在销售方式上的大部分差别。IANA 把 .computer 列为 2013 年 12 月 12 日委派的通用顶级域，赞助组织为 Binky Moon, LLC——Identity Digital 持有几十个关键词后缀的载体——因此它作为目录的一部分被销售，而非单独推广。.tech 于 2015 年 3 月 12 日委派给 Radix Technologies Inc.，一家在开曼群岛注册的注册局，技术联系人为 Tucows；Radix 自称新域名领域最大的组合注册局，在官网把 .tech 与 .online、.store、.site 并列放在最前面，并展示使用它的消费电子与机器人品牌。主动营销后缀的注册局让它在买家和投资人眼前保持可见，这是 .tech 在创业圈被认出而 .computer 没有的重要原因。场景拆分：真正与电脑打交道的生意——维修店、翻新商、复古计算社区、硬件教育——完整单词是诚实的标签 → .computer；想要最宽、最知名的创业后缀的任何科技品牌，从应用到机器人 → .tech。",
      pickA: ["电脑维修与上门服务", "装机与硬件定制工作室", "二手电脑与翻新交易", "「地名 + .computer」本地获客", "维修店、翻新商与复古计算社区"],
      pickB: ["科技创业公司", "开发者社区与黑客马拉松", "科技媒体与博客", "圈内认知度高的行业泛称", "想要 Radix 主推后缀的创业公司"],
    },
    en: {
      title: ".computer vs .tech: The Machine Itself or The Industry at Large",
      metaDescription:
        ".computer points concretely at the machine — repair shops, custom builds and refurbishing; .tech is the industry's umbrella suffix for startups and developer communities. Compare semantics, pricing and fit, then hunt names available on both.",
      verdict:
        "Both are tech words; one names the machine, the other the industry. .computer is concrete and literal — repair shops and on-site services, custom-build and hardware studios, used and refurbished dealers, and computer-skills training read plainly on name.computer, and place + .computer names (think brooklyn.computer) work as local acquisition copy for a repair shop; indie developers also wear it for retro-computing personal sites. At about {{price:computer:first:usd}} to register and {{price:computer:renew:usd}}/yr to renew, the price structure is healthy — mid first year, gentle renewal, easy to hold long-term. .tech is the industry umbrella — startups, developer communities, hackathons and tech media read on name.tech, where the suffix itself is the industry statement, with recognition proven by real adoption like ces.tech; registration is cheap but renewals run well above the first-year promo, so do the math before holding long-term. The test: if the business orbits the machine — fixing, building, selling, teaching → .computer's concrete read beats the umbrella and speaks plainer to local customers; if it's tech at large — software, internet, startups → .tech's category recognition fits better. Note .computer is narrow, so software and internet products read misplaced on it; conversely .tech is less on the nose than .computer for a hardware-repair shop. Naming: place/service + .computer for local hardware businesses; brand + .tech for technology companies. Ownership explains much of the difference in how the two are sold. IANA lists .computer as a generic TLD delegated on 12 December 2013 and sponsored by Binky Moon, LLC, the Identity Digital vehicle that holds dozens of keyword suffixes, so it is marketed as part of a catalogue rather than on its own. .tech was delegated on 12 March 2015 to Radix Technologies Inc., a Cayman Islands-incorporated registry with Tucows as technical contact; Radix describes itself as the largest portfolio registry for new domains and puts .tech front and centre on its own site alongside .online, .store and .site, showcasing consumer-electronics and robotics brands that use it. A registry that actively markets a suffix keeps it visible to buyers and investors, which is a large part of why .tech is recognised in startup circles while .computer is not. Use-case split: a business that literally deals in computers — repair shops, refurbishers, retro-computing communities, hardware education — where the full word is an honest label → .computer; a technology brand of any kind that wants the broadest and best-known startup suffix, from apps to robotics → .tech.",
      pickA: ["Computer repair & on-site services", "Custom-build & hardware studios", "Used & refurbished dealers", "Place + .computer local acquisition", "Repair shops, refurbishers and retro-computing communities"],
      pickB: ["Tech startups", "Developer communities & hackathons", "Tech media and blogs", "The high-recognition industry umbrella", "Startups that want the suffix Radix markets hardest"],
    },
  },
